<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-4-9-antiderivatives" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Antiderivatives</title>
  <!-- Stewart 4.9 . Tue Sep 8 . Skills 6-8 . Built from Handout 4 (Antiderivatives) in the
       conversational handout voice. Written 2026-09-11.
       NOTATION QUARANTINE: no integral sign anywhere in this section. It enters in 5.2
       (definite) and the indefinite form in 5.4. Antiderivatives are written F with F' = f.
       Keep the car example exactly as in Handout 4: v(t) = 3t^2, s(0) = 0, s(2) = 8. -->

  <introduction>
    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-question-antiderivatives">
      <title>Today's Question</title>
      <p>
        If we know how something is changing, can we recover the thing itself?
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
      For most of your calculus life, you have traveled in one direction:
      <md>
        \text{function} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{derivative}.
      </md>
      Today we turn around:
      <md>
        \text{derivative} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{original function?}
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Why would anyone care? Suppose you know a car's velocity, but you want its position.
      Or you know how fast water is leaking from a tank, but you want to know how much has been lost.
    </p>
    <p>
      In both cases, you know a <em>rate of change</em>, and you want the quantity that produced it.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-4-9-backward">
    <title>Can we run a derivative backward?</title>

    <p>
      Start with something familiar. Suppose <m>F'(x) = 3x^2</m>.
      Without any new formula, can you find a function <m>F</m> that works?
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-find-one-f" label="check-find-one-f">
      <title>Find one</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Which function has derivative <m>3x^2</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p><m>6x</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>That is the derivative <em>of</em> <m>3x^2</m>. We need to go the other way.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>x^3</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Yes. Check it: the derivative of <m>x^3</m> is <m>3x^2</m>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p><m>3x^3</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Check by differentiating: you get <m>9x^2</m>, not <m>3x^2</m>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p><m>\dfrac{x^3}{3}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Check by differentiating: you get <m>x^2</m>. Close, but the <m>3</m> went missing.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Easy enough. But now differentiate each of these:
      <md>
        x^3, \qquad x^3 + 4, \qquad x^3 - 10, \qquad x^3 + 100.
      </md>
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="notice-same-derivative">
      <title>What do you notice?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all four derivatives. What do you notice?
          So does knowing <m>F'(x) = 3x^2</m> pin down exactly one function?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          All four have the same derivative: <m>3x^2</m>. The constant disappears when you differentiate.
          So no. Knowing <m>F'(x) = 3x^2</m> does not tell us which one we started with.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-4-9-family">
    <title>What did the derivative forget?</title>

    <p>
      Let's look at the whole family <m>F(x) = x^3 + C</m> at once.
      Drag the <m>C</m> slider. Then drag the <m>x</m> slider and watch the slope.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-antiderivative-family">
      <caption>Gray curves: a few members of the family <m>x^3 + C</m>. Blue: the one you picked.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-antiderivative-family" label="interactive-antiderivative-family"
                   platform="jsxgraph" width="100%" aspect="4:3"
                   source="jsxgraph/antiderivative-family.js">
        <slate xml:id="interactive-antiderivative-family-slate" surface="jsxboard" aspect="4:3"/>
        <instructions>
          <p>
            The <m>C</m> slider picks a member of the family. The <m>x</m> slider moves the red
            point, and the green line is the tangent line there. Does moving <m>C</m> change the slope?
          </p>
        </instructions>
      </interactive>
    </figure>

    <exercise xml:id="notice-family">
      <title>Discuss with a neighbor</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>What changes when you move <m>C</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What stays the same about the shape?</p></li>
            <li><p>At a fixed <m>x</m>, how do the slopes of the different curves compare?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
          Then finish the sentence: the derivative remembers the ________ but forgets the ________.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>The curve slides up and down.</p></li>
            <li><p>The shape does not change at all. Every curve is the same curve, shifted.</p></li>
            <li><p>They are identical. The slope depends on <m>x</m>, not on <m>C</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
          The derivative remembers the <em>shape</em> (the slope at every point) but forgets the
          <em>vertical position</em>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-4-9-name">
    <title>Now give the idea a name</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-antiderivative">
      <title>Antiderivative</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A function <m>F</m> is an <term>antiderivative</term> of <m>f</m> on an interval if
          <m>F'(x) = f(x)</m> for every <m>x</m> in that interval.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      So <m>x^3</m> is <em>an</em> antiderivative of <m>3x^2</m>. So is <m>x^3 + 4</m>.
      So is <m>x^3 - 10</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      To describe all of them at once, we write the <term>most general antiderivative</term>:
      <m>F(x) = x^3 + C</m>, where <m>C</m> can be any constant.
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="fact-family-principle">
      <title>The family principle</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>F</m> is one antiderivative of <m>f</m> on an interval, then every antiderivative
          of <m>f</m> on that interval has the form <m>F(x) + C</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </fact>

    <p>
      Why must we include the <m>C</m>? Because leaving it off throws away most of the family.
      You would be naming one curve, when the derivative only told you the shape.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="remark-antiderivative-notation">
      <title>A note on notation</title>
      <p>
        You may have seen antiderivatives written with a special symbol somewhere.
        We are not using it yet, on purpose. That symbol has a deeper meaning,
        and we want you to see where it comes from first. It shows up in
        <xref ref="ch-integrals"/>. For now, we write <m>F</m> with <m>F' = f</m>,
        and that is all we need.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-4-9-rules-backward">
    <title>Running the derivative rules backward</title>

    <p>
      Start with the Power Rule. Differentiating <m>x^{n+1}</m> does two things:
      it multiplies by the exponent, then lowers the exponent by one.
      <md>
        \frac{d}{dx}\left[x^{n+1}\right] = (n+1)x^n.
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      To go backward, undo both steps in reverse order.
      Raise the exponent by one. Then divide by the new exponent.
      <md>
        x^n \;\longrightarrow\; \frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C, \qquad n \neq -1.
      </md>
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-power-backward-why">
      <title>Why does this work?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Differentiate <m>\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C</m>. Do you get back <m>x^n</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            \frac{d}{dx}\left[\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C\right] = \frac{(n+1)x^n}{n+1} + 0 = x^n.
          </md>
          Yes. The <m>n+1</m> cancels, and the constant disappears.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>The one exception</title>
      <p>
        What happens when <m>n = -1</m>? The formula would divide by <m>n + 1 = 0</m>. That breaks.
      </p>
      <p>
        So for <m>x^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{x}</m>, we lean on a derivative we already know:
        <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\big[\ln|x|\big] = \dfrac{1}{x}</m>.
        So an antiderivative of <m>\dfrac{1}{x}</m> is <m>\ln|x| + C</m>,
        on any interval that does not contain <m>0</m>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <exercise xml:id="toolkit-backward">
      <title>Build the toolkit yourself</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Every derivative you know is secretly an antiderivative too.
          Find the most general antiderivative of each function. Then open the solution.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>x^5</m>, &#160; <m>x^{-3}</m>, &#160; <m>x</m>, &#160; <m>\dfrac{1}{x}</m>, &#160;
          <m>e^x</m>, &#160; <m>\sin x</m>, &#160; <m>\cos x</m>, &#160; <m>\sec^2 x</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes">
            <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
            <cell>Most general antiderivative</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell><m>x^5</m></cell><cell><m>\dfrac{x^6}{6} + C</m></cell></row>
          <row><cell><m>x^{-3}</m></cell><cell><m>\dfrac{x^{-2}}{-2} + C = -\dfrac{1}{2x^2} + C</m></cell></row>
          <row><cell><m>x</m></cell><cell><m>\dfrac{x^2}{2} + C</m></cell></row>
          <row><cell><m>\dfrac{1}{x}</m></cell><cell><m>\ln|x| + C</m></cell></row>
          <row><cell><m>e^x</m></cell><cell><m>e^x + C</m></cell></row>
          <row><cell><m>\sin x</m></cell><cell><m>-\cos x + C</m></cell></row>
          <row><cell><m>\cos x</m></cell><cell><m>\sin x + C</m></cell></row>
          <row><cell><m>\sec^2 x</m></cell><cell><m>\tan x + C</m></cell></row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          The one people miss is <m>\sin x</m>. The derivative of <m>\cos x</m> is
          <m>-\sin x</m>, so we need the minus sign: <m>-\cos x</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Two more rules come along for free, because they work the same way for derivatives:
      constants stay put, and sums split into pieces.
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="fact-antiderivative-toolkit">
      <title>The antiderivative toolkit</title>
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>F' = f</m> and <m>G' = g</m>, and <m>c</m> is a constant, then:</p>
        <md>
          <mrow> c\,f(x) &amp;\;\longrightarrow\; c\,F(x) + C </mrow>
          <mrow> f(x) \pm g(x) &amp;\;\longrightarrow\; F(x) \pm G(x) + C </mrow>
          <mrow> x^n &amp;\;\longrightarrow\; \frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C \quad (n \neq -1) </mrow>
          <mrow> \frac{1}{x} &amp;\;\longrightarrow\; \ln|x| + C </mrow>
          <mrow> e^x &amp;\;\longrightarrow\; e^x + C </mrow>
          <mrow> \sin x &amp;\;\longrightarrow\; -\cos x + C </mrow>
          <mrow> \cos x &amp;\;\longrightarrow\; \sin x + C </mrow>
          <mrow> \sec^2 x &amp;\;\longrightarrow\; \tan x + C </mrow>
        </md>
      </statement>
    </fact>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Best error check today</title>
      <p>Differentiate your answer. If you get back the function you started with, you are right.</p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-4-9-whole-family">
    <title>Finding the whole family</title>

    <example xml:id="example-antiderivative-polynomial">
      <title>We do: one term at a time</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Find the most general antiderivative of <m>f(x) = 6x^2 - 4x + 5</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Work backward one term at a time. Raise the exponent, divide by the new exponent.
          <md>
            <mrow> 6x^2 &amp;\;\longrightarrow\; 6 \cdot \frac{x^3}{3} = 2x^3 </mrow>
            <mrow> -4x &amp;\;\longrightarrow\; -4 \cdot \frac{x^2}{2} = -2x^2 </mrow>
            <mrow> 5 &amp;\;\longrightarrow\; 5x </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          One <m>+C</m> covers the whole thing: <m>F(x) = 2x^3 - 2x^2 + 5x + C</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Check.</alert> <m>F'(x) = 6x^2 - 4x + 5</m>. It matches.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <exercise xml:id="try-antiderivative-polynomial">
      <title>Try one</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the most general antiderivative of <m>f(x) = 4x - 3x^2</m>.
          Check your answer by differentiating <em>before</em> you open the solution.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>F(x) = 2x^2 - x^3 + C</m>. Check: <m>F'(x) = 4x - 3x^2</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-4-9-initial-conditions">
    <title>A family is not always enough</title>

    <p>
      Suppose <m>F'(x) = 3x^2</m>. Then <m>F(x) = x^3 + C</m>. A whole family.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now suppose we are also told <m>F(1) = 5</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      On a graph, that says: pick the one member of the family that passes through <m>(1, 5)</m>.
      Go back to the family graph above. Set <m>x = 1</m>, then hunt with the <m>C</m> slider.
    </p>
    <p>
      In algebra, plug in <m>x = 1</m>: <m>\;5 = 1^3 + C</m>.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-find-c" label="check-find-c">
      <title>Pick the family member</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>C =</m> <fillin answer="4" mode="number"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>5 = 1 + C</m>, so <m>C = 4</m>, and the one function we want is <m>F(x) = x^3 + 4</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Remember</title>
      <p>Derivative information <m>+</m> one condition <m>\longrightarrow</m> one particular function.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      A problem like this, with a derivative plus a starting value, is called an
      <term>initial value problem</term>.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-ivp-you-do" label="check-ivp-you-do">
      <title>You do: select the right member of the family</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>F</m> if <m>F'(x) = 8x^3 - 6x</m> and <m>F(0) = 7</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Step 1:</alert> find the family. <alert>Step 2:</alert> use the condition to find <m>C</m>.
          <alert>Step 3:</alert> check both conditions.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>C =</m> <fillin answer="7" mode="number"/>
          &#160;&#160;&#160; and then <m>F(1) =</m> <fillin answer="6" mode="number"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. The family is <m>F(x) = 2x^4 - 3x^2 + C</m>, and <m>F(0) = C = 7</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>F(x) = 2x^4 - 3x^2 + 7</m>, so <m>F(1) = 2 - 3 + 7 = 6</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>What if we are given the second derivative?</title>
      <p>
        Then we have to run backward <em>twice</em>. And every trip backward brings its own constant.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <exercise xml:id="check-second-derivative-ivp" label="check-second-derivative-ivp">
      <title>Two steps back</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>f''(x) = 12x^2 + 6x - 4</m>, with <m>f(0) = 4</m> and <m>f(1) = 1</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          First recover <m>f'(x) = 4x^3 + 3x^2 - 4x + C</m>.
          Then go back again: <m>f(x) = x^4 + x^3 - 2x^2 + Cx + D</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now use the two conditions.
          <m>D =</m> <fillin answer="4" mode="number"/>
          &#160;&#160;&#160; <m>C =</m> <fillin answer="-3" mode="number"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. Every term except <m>D</m> vanishes at <m>x = 0</m>, so <m>f(0) = D = 4</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>f(1) = 1 + 1 - 2 + C + 4 = 4 + C</m>. Setting that equal to <m>1</m> gives <m>C = -3</m>. So <m>f(x) = x^4 + x^3 - 2x^2 - 3x + 4</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Why two constants? Each time we undo a derivative, we lose track of one vertical shift.
      Two derivatives, two lost shifts, two conditions to pin them down.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-4-9-without-formula">
    <title>Can we recover a function without a formula?</title>

    <p>
      This is an important shift. Sometimes there is no formula, only a graph.
    </p>
    <p>
      Below is the graph of <m>y = f(x)</m>, and <m>F' = f</m>.
      So the graph of <m>f</m> tells you the <em>slope</em> of <m>F</m>. Also, <m>F(0) = 2</m>.
      Leave the checkbox alone for now.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-graph-to-antiderivative">
      <caption>The graph of <m>f</m>. It gives the slope of <m>F</m> at every <m>x</m>.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-graph-to-antiderivative" label="interactive-graph-to-antiderivative"
                   platform="jsxgraph" width="100%" aspect="4:3"
                   source="jsxgraph/graph-to-antiderivative.js">
        <slate xml:id="interactive-graph-to-antiderivative-slate" surface="jsxboard" aspect="4:3"/>
        <instructions>
          <p>
            Answer the questions below first. Then check the box to see one possible <m>F</m>
            and compare it with your sketch.
          </p>
        </instructions>
      </interactive>
    </figure>

    <exercise xml:id="notice-graph-to-antiderivative">
      <title>Read the slopes</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Where is <m>F</m> increasing?</p></li>
            <li><p>Where is <m>F</m> decreasing?</p></li>
            <li><p>Where does <m>F</m> have horizontal tangents?</p></li>
            <li><p>Where should <m>F</m> have a local minimum? A local maximum?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
          Now sketch one possible <m>F</m> on paper, starting at the point <m>(0, 2)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p><m>F</m> increases where <m>f \gt 0</m>: between <m>x = 1</m> and <m>x = 3</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>F</m> decreases where <m>f \lt 0</m>: from <m>0</m> to <m>1</m>, and from <m>3</m> to <m>5</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Where <m>f = 0</m>: at <m>x = 1</m> and <m>x = 3</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>A local minimum at <m>x = 1</m> (decreasing, then increasing) and a local maximum at <m>x = 3</m> (increasing, then decreasing).</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Reading a graph of the slope</title>
      <p>If <m>F' = f</m>, then <m>f \gt 0</m> means <m>F</m> increases, and <m>f \lt 0</m> means <m>F</m> decreases.</p>
      <p>Zeros of <m>f</m> are where <m>F</m> has horizontal tangents.</p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-4-9-car">
    <title>Our running example: a car</title>

    <p>
      Now back to a physical situation. A car has velocity <m>v(t) = 3t^2</m> meters per second.
      It starts at position <m>s(0) = 0</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Velocity is the derivative of position: <m>s'(t) = v(t) = 3t^2</m>.
      So position is an antiderivative of velocity.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Recover the family.</alert> <m>s(t) = t^3 + C</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Use the starting position.</alert> <m>s(0) = 0^3 + C = 0</m>, so <m>C = 0</m> and <m>s(t) = t^3</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      So where is the car at <m>t = 2</m> seconds?
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-car-position" label="check-car-position">
      <title>Where is the car?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>s(2) =</m> <fillin answer="8" mode="number"/> meters
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>s(2) = 2^3 = 8</m>. Since the car started at <m>0</m>, it moved <m>8</m> meters.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-remember-8">
      <title>Remember this number: 8</title>
      <p>The car traveled <m>s(2) - s(0) = 8</m> meters.</p>
      <p>We will meet this same car again.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>From acceleration all the way to position</title>
      <p>
        Velocity is the derivative of position. Acceleration is the derivative of velocity.
        So we can run backward twice:
        <md>
          a(t) \;\xrightarrow{\text{antidifferentiate}}\; v(t) \;\xrightarrow{\text{antidifferentiate}}\; s(t).
        </md>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <exercise xml:id="check-acceleration-to-position" label="check-acceleration-to-position">
      <title>You do: a particle</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A particle has acceleration <m>a(t) = 6t + 4</m>. Its initial velocity is
          <m>v(0) = -6</m> and its initial position is <m>s(0) = 9</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Step 1:</alert> recover <m>v(t)</m> and use <m>v(0) = -6</m>.
          <alert>Step 2:</alert> recover <m>s(t)</m> and use <m>s(0) = 9</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>v(1) =</m> <fillin answer="1" mode="number"/>
          &#160;&#160;&#160; <m>s(1) =</m> <fillin answer="6" mode="number"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>v(t) = 3t^2 + 4t + C</m>, and <m>v(0) = -6</m> gives <m>v(t) = 3t^2 + 4t - 6</m>. So <m>v(1) = 1</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>s(t) = t^3 + 2t^2 - 6t + D</m>, and <m>s(0) = 9</m> gives <m>s(t) = t^3 + 2t^2 - 6t + 9</m>. So <m>s(1) = 6</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-4-9-practice">
    <title>Putting it together</title>

    <exercise xml:id="less-scaffolding-antiderivatives">
      <title>Less scaffolding</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Find the most general antiderivative of <m>f(x) = 5x^4 - 3x^2 + 7</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find <m>F</m> if <m>F'(x) = 4x^3 + 2x</m> and <m>F(1) = 3</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>A particle has velocity <m>v(t) = 6t - 4</m> and <m>s(0) = 10</m>. Find its position function.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p><m>F(x) = x^5 - x^3 + 7x + C</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>The family is <m>x^4 + x^2 + C</m>. Then <m>F(1) = 2 + C = 3</m>, so <m>F(x) = x^4 + x^2 + 1</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>s(t) = 3t^2 - 4t + 10</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="check-error-analysis-plus-c" label="check-error-analysis-plus-c">
      <title>Error analysis: who is most general?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A class is asked for the most general antiderivative of <m>F'(x) = 4x^3</m>.
          Three students answer. Who gives the <em>most general</em> answer?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p>Student A: <m>F(x) = x^4</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Student A found <em>an</em> antiderivative, and it is correct. But it is only one member of the family. The <m>+C</m> is missing.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Student B: <m>F(x) = x^4 + C</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. Correct function, and the <m>+C</m> captures the whole family.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p>Student C: <m>F(x) = 4x^4 + C</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Differentiate it: you get <m>16x^3</m>. Student C raised the exponent but forgot to divide by the new exponent.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exit-ticket-antiderivatives">
      <title>Exit ticket</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>In your own words, what is an antiderivative? Explain it using a car's velocity and position.</p></li>
            <li><p>Why does the general answer contain <m>+C</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>If <m>F' = f</m> and <m>f \gt 0</m> on an interval, is <m>F</m> increasing or decreasing there?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why does an initial condition matter?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>An antiderivative of <m>f</m> is a function whose derivative is <m>f</m>. If <m>f</m> is a car's velocity, an antiderivative is its position: you recover where the car is from how fast it is going.</p></li>
            <li><p>Differentiating erases constants. So running backward can only recover the function up to a vertical shift.</p></li>
            <li><p>Increasing. The slope of <m>F</m> is positive.</p></li>
            <li><p>It picks out the one member of the family we actually want, like knowing where the car started.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Main idea</title>
      <p>Knowing a rate of change lets us work backward, but only to a family of functions.</p>
      <p>
        The derivative remembers how the function changes. It forgets the vertical position.
        An initial condition tells us which member of the family we need.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>
</section>
